window.SURGERY.sections.push({
  id: "cabg",
  category: "coronary",
  title: "On-Pump Coronary Artery Bypass Grafting",
  subtitle: "Rerouting blood around fixed coronary stenoses with durable conduit, on an arrested heart — the definitive operation for multivessel and left-main coronary disease.",
  readMins: 30,
  day: 7,
  blocks: [
    {
      title: "Start here — the coronary anatomy you forgot",
      figures: [{"id": "cabg-lita-and-vein-grafts-v2", "src": "assets/surgery-generated/cabg/lita-and-vein-grafts-v2.png", "width": 1536, "height": 1024, "alt": "Anterior heart showing an in-situ left internal thoracic artery graft to the LAD and a separate aorta-origin vein graft to a lateral coronary branch.", "caption": "The in-situ left internal thoracic artery stays connected to the subclavian artery proximally and reaches the LAD. A free saphenous vein graft instead needs a proximal aortic anastomosis."}],
      html: "<p>The heart's own blood supply comes off the aortic root through two ostia just above the aortic valve cusps. The <b>left main coronary artery</b> is short — often only a centimetre or two — and almost immediately bifurcates (sometimes trifurcates, with an intervening ramus branch) into the <b>left anterior descending (LAD)</b> artery, which runs down the <b>anterior interventricular groove</b> to supply the anterior wall, the anteroseptal septum, and the apex, and the <b>left circumflex (LCx)</b> artery, which runs in the <b>left atrioventricular groove</b> around the obtuse margin to supply the lateral wall. The <b>right coronary artery (RCA)</b> runs in the <b>right atrioventricular groove</b> and, in most people, continues around the inferior heart to become the <b>posterior descending artery (PDA)</b>, which runs in the posterior interventricular groove. Whichever artery gives rise to the PDA defines <b>coronary dominance</b>: right-dominant (from the RCA) in roughly 80 to 85 percent of people, left-dominant (from the LCx) in a minority, and codominant in the rest. Because the left main feeds both the LAD and the circumflex territories, disease there threatens the majority of the left ventricle at once — this is why left main stenosis is treated with special urgency regardless of symptoms.</p><p>The point of coronary bypass grafting is deceptively simple once you see it this way: a fixed, flow-limiting stenosis or occlusion in one of these vessels starves the myocardium it feeds, and a bypass graft simply gives that starved territory a second, unobstructed source of blood, sewn in beyond (distal to) the blockage. The operation does not fix the diseased native artery; it routes around it. Everything that follows in this article — which conduit, which target, how the anastomosis is constructed — is in service of that one idea: build a durable, high-flow conduit from a reliable inflow source to a point on the coronary artery distal to significant disease, with good native runoff beyond it to carry the flow onward.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> Coronary dominance is defined purely by which artery gives rise to the PDA (RCA = right-dominant, LCx = left-dominant), not by which vessel is \"bigger.\" Know a patient's dominance before the operation — it changes which territory the RCA versus the circumflex system is actually responsible for, and therefore which graft protects which territory.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Physiology & pathophysiology — why the LAD gets the LIMA",
      html: "<p>Two separate biological facts converge to make the <b>left internal mammary artery (LIMA) to LAD</b> graft the single most important anastomosis in cardiac surgery. First, the LAD supplies the largest and most functionally important territory of the left ventricle in most patients, so protecting it has the greatest impact on long-term survival of any single graft. Second, the LIMA is not just conduit — it is a muscular <b>artery</b> harvested in continuity with its native origin off the subclavian artery, so unlike a vein graft it retains an endothelium and vasoreactivity biologically similar to the coronary artery it is sewn to, resists intimal hyperplasia and atherosclerosis far better than vein, and has been shown repeatedly to remain patent (open and functioning) at 10 and 15 years in the great majority of patients. Put together: the highest-value target gets the most durable conduit, and that pairing — LIMA to LAD — is why it is called the <b>anchor graft</b> of a bypass operation and is placed in essentially every CABG unless the LAD itself is unsuitable.</p><p>Grafts fail for reasons that differ by conduit and by time frame, and understanding the mechanism tells you what to watch for. Early graft failure (days to weeks) is usually technical — a kink, a twist in the conduit, a poor anastomosis, or thrombosis, particularly in a small or diffusely diseased target vessel with poor distal runoff. <b>Competitive flow</b> is a specific and important physiological cause of both early dysfunction and long-term attrition: if the native coronary artery is only mildly stenosed, native antegrade flow competes with graft flow at the anastomosis, the pressure gradient across the graft is small, flow through it is sluggish, and over time it can narrow down or occlude — which is why grafts are targeted at vessels with genuinely significant stenosis (typically considered greater than 70 percent, or greater than 50 percent for the left main), not borderline lesions. Arterial grafts are additionally prone to <b>spasm</b> — a reversible smooth-muscle constriction, particularly of the radial artery, that can mimic graft failure early on and is mitigated with vasodilator therapy (commonly a calcium-channel blocker) in the perioperative period. Vein grafts fail later by a different mechanism: intimal hyperplasia and accelerated atherosclerosis over years, which is the biological reason arterial conduit has better long-term patency than vein.</p><div class=\"kbox danger\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Red flag</span> Grafting a vessel with only a mild (for example 40 to 50 percent) stenosis risks competitive flow: native flow wins the competition, graft flow stays low, and the graft is prone to accelerated closure. Target selection is not just \"is this vessel diseased\" but \"is this vessel diseased enough, with good distal runoff, to guarantee the graft will actually be needed and used.\"</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Presentation, evidence and the decision to operate",
      html: "<p>Patients come to CABG from two broad presentations: stable but anatomically severe coronary disease found on elective workup (multivessel disease, left main disease, or disease in a diabetic with multivessel involvement), or an acute coronary syndrome where anatomy is unsuitable for percutaneous intervention (PCI) alone. The decision to operate, rather than stent or treat medically, rests on a mix of anatomic complexity, comorbidity, and increasingly, trial evidence weighed by a multidisciplinary <b>Heart Team</b>.</p><p>Anatomic complexity is quantified with the <b>SYNTAX score</b>, an angiographic scoring system that grades the number, location, and complexity of coronary lesions (bifurcations, chronic total occlusions, calcification, tortuosity); a high SYNTAX score identifies disease complex enough that PCI is less likely to achieve durable, complete revascularization and tilts the Heart Team toward CABG. <b>FREEDOM</b> established that in patients with diabetes and multivessel coronary disease, CABG produces a significant long-term survival advantage over PCI, largely because surgical revascularization is more resistant to the diffuse, rapidly progressive atherosclerosis characteristic of diabetic coronary disease and because it protects territories beyond just the culprit lesion. For left main disease specifically, <b>EXCEL</b> and <b>NOBLE</b> both compared PCI with drug-eluting stents against CABG; taken together with longer-term follow-up, they showed that outcomes are more comparable for lower to intermediate anatomic complexity left main disease, while CABG maintains an edge as anatomic complexity (SYNTAX score) rises — reinforcing that the choice is anatomy- and complexity-dependent rather than one-size-fits-all. In ischemic cardiomyopathy with a severely reduced ejection fraction, <b>STICH</b> showed that CABG (with or without surgical ventricular reconstruction) provided a long-term survival benefit over medical therapy alone in patients with viable, revascularizable myocardium, cementing surgical revascularization as standard in appropriately selected patients with ischemic heart failure.</p><p>The unifying principle behind all of this evidence is <b>completeness of revascularization</b>: CABG's durability advantage comes largely from its ability to graft essentially every significantly diseased vessel in one operation, including small and distal targets that PCI cannot easily reach, whereas PCI tends to treat the most severe culprit lesions and may leave other diseased territories untouched. This is discussed and decided by the <b>Heart Team</b> — interventional cardiology and cardiac surgery reviewing the anatomy together — which is now the guideline-endorsed standard for complex or left main coronary disease.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> Hold three trial names and their one-line message: <b>SYNTAX</b> (complexity score that predicts when PCI under-delivers), <b>FREEDOM</b> (diabetes + multivessel disease -> CABG survival advantage), <b>STICH</b> (ischemic cardiomyopathy -> CABG survival benefit with viable myocardium). Add <b>EXCEL/NOBLE</b> for the nuanced, complexity-dependent left-main story.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "The operation, step by step",
      figures: [{
        id: "cabg-end-to-side-anastomosis",
        src: "assets/surgery-generated/cabg/end-to-side-anastomosis-v1.png",
        width: 1536, height: 1024,
        alt: "External and cutaway views show a bypass conduit sewn to the side of an open native coronary artery.",
        caption: "The beveled distal graft end is joined to a longitudinal coronary arteriotomy. The heel and toe form a broad, smooth opening while the native coronary lumen continues distally."
      }],
      html: "<p>After median sternotomy and systemic heparinization, conduit harvest and cannulation typically proceed in parallel: the LIMA is taken down from the chest wall (usually as a pedicled harvest, keeping the artery surrounded by its vein and fascia, or skeletonized to preserve more sternal blood supply) while, on the leg or forearm, saphenous vein or radial artery is harvested as the second conduit. The patient is cannulated (aorta and right atrium, as detailed in the bypass and sternotomy articles), goes on cardiopulmonary bypass, and the aorta is cross-clamped with cardioplegic arrest, so the anastomoses are constructed on a still, bloodless, protected heart.</p><p>Each distal anastomosis is a precise <b>end-to-side</b> join between the open end of the conduit and a small arteriotomy in the native coronary artery beyond the stenosis. The arteriotomy is fashioned longitudinally, and the graft is bevelled to create a <b>heel and toe</b> — the proximal (heel) and distal (toe) points of the elliptical opening — so that the completed anastomosis is a smooth, non-narrowing continuation of the artery rather than a stepped or kinked junction; a poorly constructed heel or toe is a common technical cause of early graft failure. On the arrested heart, the surgeon can rotate and position the heart freely to expose targets on the lateral and inferior walls that would be difficult to reach on a beating heart — one of the principal advantages of on-pump technique. Conduits may be used as simple single grafts (one conduit, one target), as <b>sequential grafts</b> (one conduit with side-to-side anastomoses to an intermediate target before a final end-to-side anastomosis to the last target, extending the reach of a single length of conduit), or as a <b>composite (Y or T) graft</b>, where a second conduit is anastomosed onto the side of the first (commonly a radial or vein segment off the side of the LIMA) rather than independently off the aorta, reducing the number of proximal aortic anastomoses.</p><p>After each distal anastomosis (for grafts not arising from the LIMA in situ), the <b>proximal anastomosis</b> is constructed onto the ascending aorta, either under a single partial-occluding side-biting clamp after the cross-clamp is released, or, in some cases, all proximals are done under the single cross-clamp before releasing it, depending on surgeon technique and aortic quality. Once all anastomoses are complete and the cross-clamp is off and the heart reperfused, <b>transit-time flow measurement</b> is used on each graft before closing: an ultrasonic probe placed around the conduit reports mean flow together with a <b>pulsatility index</b> (a measure of flow variability across the cardiac cycle) and the pattern of <b>diastolic filling</b> — a graft with low, non-pulsatile, or predominantly systolic flow (rather than the expected diastolic-dominant coronary flow pattern) suggests a technical problem (kink, twist, poor target, competitive flow) and prompts immediate re-inspection or revision before the chest is closed, while the problem is still fixable.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> Transit-time flow measurement is done in the operating room, before closure, precisely because a technically imperfect anastomosis is far easier and safer to revise on the table than to discover on a postoperative troponin rise or angiogram. A low pulsatility index and good diastolic-dominant flow are the two numbers to know are reassuring; a flat or systolic-dominant tracing is not.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Conduit choice — the second most important decision after the LIMA",
      html: "<p>With the LIMA essentially fixed as the graft to the LAD, the second decision — what to use for the remaining targets — has shifted meaningfully over the last decade. The <b>great saphenous vein</b> remains the most commonly used conduit for its ease of harvest and abundant length, but as a vein used as an artery it is exposed to arterial pressure it was never built for, and over years it develops intimal hyperplasia and accelerated atherosclerosis, giving it inferior long-term patency compared with arterial conduit. The <b>radial artery</b> has become the preferred second conduit for the next most important target in appropriately selected patients: the <b>2021 ACC/AHA/SCAI guidelines</b> and pooled evidence from the <b>RADIAL meta-analysis (patient-level pooling of the major randomized trials)</b> both support radial artery graft patency and clinical outcomes superior to saphenous vein for grafting a significantly stenosed target, provided the target vessel has a high-grade stenosis (again, competitive flow undermines an arterial graft to a mildly diseased vessel more than it does a vein). The radial artery is not used if the target stenosis is only moderate, in the presence of severe forearm arterial disease or a positive Allen's test suggesting inadequate collateral hand perfusion, and it is prone to spasm, generally managed with a perioperative calcium-channel blocker or other vasodilator protocol.</p><p><b>No-touch vein harvest</b> — taking the saphenous vein with a cuff of surrounding perivascular tissue rather than skeletonizing it bare, and handling it without distending it under high pressure — preserves the vein's own vasa vasorum and endothelial function and has been associated with improved long-term vein graft patency compared with conventionally harvested vein, approaching the durability more typical of arterial conduit. <b>Bilateral internal mammary artery (BIMA)</b> grafting, using both the left and right internal thoracic arteries, extends the survival and patency benefit of arterial grafting to a second target (commonly the diagonal or circumflex system) and was studied specifically in the <b>ART (Arterial Revascularization Trial)</b>; the tradeoff, as covered in the sternotomy article, is a meaningfully higher risk of deep sternal wound infection with bilateral harvest, especially in diabetic and obese patients, which is why BIMA is used selectively rather than universally and, when used, is often harvested skeletonized to preserve sternal perfusion.</p><div class=\"kbox mnemonic\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Mnemonic</span> Conduit durability ranking, best to worst for long-term patency — <b>LIMA in situ > radial artery (to a high-grade stenosis) > right ITA > saphenous vein (conventional)</b> — with no-touch vein harvest narrowing that last gap.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Target selection, routing and the technical details that matter",
      figures: [{
        id: "cabg-graft-routing",
        src: "assets/surgery-generated/cabg/graft-routing-v1.png",
        width: 1774, height: 887,
        alt: "Three bypass conduits over the heart compare a gentle route, an acute kink, and an excessively taut straight course.",
        caption: "A bypass conduit needs a smooth route and enough length to accommodate heart movement. An acute kink or excessive tension can compromise flow despite an open distal anastomosis."
      }],
      html: "<p>A vessel is chosen as a bypass target when it has a haemodynamically significant stenosis, is of adequate calibre (generally at least about 1.5 mm, though smaller vessels can be grafted by experienced hands), and has reasonable distal runoff — that is, the artery beyond the anastomosis is not itself diffusely diseased down to nothing, because a graft delivering flow into a dead end will fail regardless of how well the anastomosis itself is constructed. Once the anastomosis is complete, the conduit must be <b>routed</b> without tension, twist, or kink on the beating, reperfused, and eventually closing heart — a straight, appropriately lengthed lie from the aorta (or from the LIMA's native origin) to the target, checked by watching the graft as the heart is manipulated and the chest is prepared for closure, because a graft that looks perfect on the arrested, decompressed heart can kink once the heart re-fills and returns to its normal position and size.</p><p>Manipulating the ascending aorta — for cannulation, cross-clamping, side-biting clamping for proximal anastomoses, and any additional partial clamping — carries a real risk of dislodging atheromatous debris into the cerebral or systemic circulation, particularly in a heavily calcified or atheromatous (\"porcelain\" in its most severe form) aorta; this <b>embolic risk from aortic manipulation</b> is a major driver of perioperative stroke in on-pump CABG and is precisely the risk that off-pump and anaortic techniques (covered in the companion OPCAB article) are designed to reduce. Epiaortic ultrasound scanning of the ascending aorta before cannulation, to identify high-risk atheroma and guide or avoid clamp placement, is an important adjunct in patients at elevated stroke risk.</p><div class=\"kbox danger\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Red flag</span> A heavily calcified or diffusely atheromatous ascending aorta on preoperative imaging or intraoperative palpation should prompt epiaortic ultrasound and a deliberate plan to minimize aortic manipulation (fewer clamp applications, a composite/Y-graft to reduce proximal anastomoses, or an anaortic off-pump approach) rather than proceeding with routine multiple clamping.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Complications & how to manage them",
      html: "<p>Perioperative myocardial infarction after CABG is diagnosed by a combination of new ECG changes, regional wall motion abnormality, and a substantial rise in cardiac biomarkers (troponin) beyond the expected postoperative elevation from the surgery itself, and its most common cause is a technical problem with a specific graft — an occluded or severely stenosed anastomosis, a kinked or twisted conduit, or embolization of air or debris down a coronary, most often the <b>right coronary artery</b> because of its anterior take-off from the aortic root, which makes it the natural collecting point for air during de-airing. When perioperative MI is suspected, urgent coronary angiography (or a return to the operating room if angiography is not immediately available and index of suspicion is high) is used to identify and, where possible, revise or re-graft the culprit vessel, because early recognition and correction meaningfully changes outcome.</p><p><b>Postoperative atrial fibrillation</b> is the single most common complication after CABG, typically arising on postoperative day two or three as pericardial inflammation, autonomic shifts, and electrolyte derangement (particularly hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia) create a substrate for atrial re-entry; management is rate or rhythm control, electrolyte repletion, and anticoagulation decisions individualized to the patient's stroke risk and bleeding risk, and it usually resolves as the acute postoperative inflammatory state settles, though it is associated with a higher risk of stroke and longer hospital stay. <b>Perioperative stroke</b> arises predominantly from atheroembolism during aortic manipulation, as above, or, less commonly, from hypoperfusion or atrial fibrillation-related cardioembolism; the mitigations are the epiaortic screening, minimized aortic touch, and prompt AF management already described. <b>Deep sternal wound infection</b> after CABG follows the same risk factors, microbiology and management principles covered in the sternotomy article, with bilateral ITA harvest in a diabetic being the classic high-risk combination. Early graft failure presents as recurrent angina, ECG changes, or haemodynamic instability in the immediate postoperative period and, as above, is a technical problem until proven otherwise.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> New inferior ST changes or a new RV dysfunction pattern in the hours after CABG should immediately raise the possibility of RCA graft problems (air embolism or a technical issue with that specific anastomosis), because the right coronary is the classic target of intraoperative air embolization given its anterior ostial position.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Pimping ceiling — the fine print",
      html: "<p>Know the numeric thresholds cold: a stenosis is generally considered significant enough to graft at <b>greater than 70 percent</b> diameter narrowing for most epicardial vessels, and <b>greater than 50 percent</b> for the left main, given the disproportionate myocardial territory it protects. Know the <b>SYNTAX score</b> conceptually as a lesion-complexity score (not merely a stenosis-severity score) that incorporates number of lesions, bifurcation involvement, chronic total occlusions, calcification, and tortuosity, and that a higher score progressively favours CABG over PCI for equivalent anatomic disease. Know that <b>FREEDOM</b> is the trial to cite for diabetes plus multivessel disease, that <b>STICH</b> is the trial to cite for ischemic cardiomyopathy with viable myocardium, and that <b>EXCEL and NOBLE</b> are the trials to cite for left main disease specifically, with their combined message being complexity-dependent equipoise rather than a uniform winner.</p><p>Know the mechanics of graft assessment: <b>transit-time flow measurement</b> gives a mean flow, a <b>pulsatility index</b> (lower is better; a very high pulsatility index suggests high downstream resistance or a technical problem), and a qualitative flow-waveform pattern, where healthy coronary grafts show <b>diastolic-dominant flow</b> because native coronary perfusion itself is predominantly diastolic (driven by the pressure differential when the aortic valve is closed and myocardial compression is lowest). Know why <b>competitive flow</b> undermines grafts to mildly stenosed vessels: with two open pathways (native artery and graft) feeding the same distal bed, the path of least resistance wins, and a healthy native artery simply out-competes a new graft for flow, predisposing the ungrafted-in-practice conduit to string sign or occlusion over time. And know the conduit hierarchy and its evidence: LIMA-to-LAD as the fixed anchor graft with the best published patency (routinely quoted in excess of 90 percent at 10 years), radial artery over vein for the next important high-grade target per <b>2021 ACC/AHA/SCAI</b> guidance and the <b>RADIAL meta-analysis</b>, BIMA's survival and patency benefit weighed against its sternal infection cost per <b>ART</b>, and no-touch harvest as a technique that narrows the durability gap for saphenous vein.</p><div class=\"kbox mnemonic\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Mnemonic</span> The trial quartet for CABG indications — <b>\"SYNTAX sizes it up, FREEDOM is for diabetics, STICH is for sick ventricles, EXCEL/NOBLE argue over the left main.\"</b></div>"
    }
  ],
  steps: [
    "Perform median sternotomy and confirm the operative plan, conduit strategy and target vessels against the coronary angiogram",
    "Harvest the LIMA (pedicled or skeletonized) and, in parallel, harvest saphenous vein and/or radial artery as additional conduit",
    "Heparinize, confirm ACT, and cannulate the aorta and right atrium for cardiopulmonary bypass",
    "Go on bypass, cross-clamp the ascending aorta and deliver cardioplegia to arrest the heart",
    "Expose and open the first target coronary artery beyond its stenosis with a longitudinal arteriotomy",
    "Bevel the conduit to create a heel and toe and construct the end-to-side (or sequential side-to-side) distal anastomosis",
    "Repeat distal anastomoses in planned order for each remaining target, rotating and positioning the arrested heart as needed for exposure",
    "Construct proximal anastomoses onto the ascending aorta for grafts not arising from the LIMA in situ (or a composite Y/T anastomosis onto another graft)",
    "De-air the heart under TEE guidance before releasing the cross-clamp",
    "Release the cross-clamp, reperfuse the heart, and restore a perfusing rhythm with pacing wires tested and available",
    "Perform transit-time flow measurement on every graft and revise any anastomosis with abnormal flow or pulsatility",
    "Wean and separate from cardiopulmonary bypass once rewarmed, de-aired and haemodynamically ready",
    "Give protamine slowly with the pump suckers off and achieve haemostasis, checking all graft and cannulation sites",
    "Place mediastinal (and pleural, if opened) drains and epicardial pacing wires",
    "Close the sternum with wires (or plate fixation if indicated) and close the soft tissue in layers",
    "Monitor postoperatively for perioperative MI, atrial fibrillation, bleeding and sternal wound complications"
  ]
});

